O='OBJ-6136-3.1.05-'; P='OBJ-6136-10.1.08-00'
claims={
 '3.1':[
  (O+'00','Money capital finances production, while capital goods are produced assets used in production. A choice about finance and equipment depends on the task and the resources already available.','comparison'),
  (O+'01','Money capital is finance available to acquire productive resources. Spending it on a tool converts available finance into a productive asset; it does not make the finance and the tool the same thing.','definition'),
  (O+'02','Capital goods are produced assets used to make goods or services, such as a sewing machine or a workbench. Their productive use distinguishes them from money held to finance purchases.','definition')],
 '10.1':[(P,'A pilot study is a small trial of research procedures before the main enquiry. The researcher checks how the instrument works with relevant participants, records difficulties, changes the procedure and checks that the revision addresses the problem.','procedural')]
}
texts={
 '3.1':[
  ('comparison','Finance and productive assets',
   'Money capital is finance available for production. Capital goods are produced assets used in production. A sewing group might hold N$600 in a cash box and use a sewing machine to make school bags. The cash can finance cloth, transport or equipment. The machine performs a productive task. Counting both as the same kind of resource hides the decision the group faces: having a machine does not mean it has money for cloth, and having money does not mean it already has a suitable machine.',[0,1,2]),
  ('worked_application','A choice with a reason',
   'Consider an invented workshop near Otjiwarongo. Its machine works, but production has stopped because there is no cloth. A new machine would add productive equipment without removing that immediate shortage. Finance for cloth would allow the existing equipment to be used. If the machine instead breaks and cannot be repaired, the priority changes: the workshop needs access to replacement equipment as well as the money or credit to obtain it. The practical choice follows the bottleneck, rather than a rule that money or machines are more useful in every setting.',[0,1,2]),
  ('interpretation','Reading evidence about equipment',
   'An invented sewing group records output before and after obtaining a machine. In week one it makes 30 bags using hand tools; in week two it makes 50 using the machine. In this example the equipment is associated with 20 additional bags. That comparison alone does not prove the machine caused the whole change: the hours worked, worker numbers, material supply and type of bag must also be comparable. A careful description first identifies the change, then states what the evidence can and cannot establish. More equipment is useful where it fits the productive task and can be operated and maintained.',[2,0])],
 '10.1':[
  ('process','Trying a questionnaire before the main enquiry',
   'A pilot study is a small preliminary trial. It helps a researcher discover practical problems before using the procedure throughout an enquiry. A learner researching journeys to a community water point can try a draft questionnaire with a few people similar to the intended respondents. The learner checks whether they understand the questions, whether response categories fit their experiences and whether the instrument records the information needed for the research aim. The trial should help improve the procedure; repeating a definition of a pilot study does not describe what the researcher actually did.',[0]),
  ('worked_application','A wording problem and its repair',
   'In an invented enquiry, six volunteers try a question asking whether their water journey takes a long time. Four ask what long means. The phrase gives them no common measurement rule, so their answers may not be comparable. The learner records that difficulty and replaces the question with a request for the number of minutes for a return journey, including waiting. A second small trial checks whether the new instruction is understood and can be answered. This is a change to the instrument prompted by evidence, rather than a claim that a small trial guarantees reliable results.',[0]),
  ('limitation','What the trial does not establish',
   'Six volunteers can reveal confusing wording, but their answers cannot establish the travel-time pattern for a whole settlement. The pilot sample is chosen to test procedures, while the main sampling plan must address the enquiry population and aim. Record what changed and why, then describe the revised collection method clearly enough for another learner to follow it. In the final report, distinguish findings from the main collection from observations made while testing the instrument. If the trial identifies a sensitive question, revise the approach with the supervising teacher before the main collection.',[0])]
}
